{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbv8UYpeZBaAfQ2CHUQ9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7546, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7546}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "db55d650-87ba-4dac-bf12-918801478d1e", "uuid": "6ad6c70f-f0bf-4422-a4b8-0b32ffc5953b", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T17:32:21.063Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbv8UDyJoiZMSPMf2f5j"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbv8UYpeZBaAfQ2CHUQ9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "`git rebase --onto` lets you choose a new base for a range of commits. A plain `git rebase` assumes \"my commits are everything since I forked from upstream\", but with `--onto` you say which commits to move and where to put them.\n\n## Syntax\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> [<branch>]\n```\n\nGit takes the commits that are in `<branch>` but not in `<upstream>` (the range `upstream..branch`), replays them on top of `<newbase>`, and then points `<branch>` at the result. If you leave out `<branch>`, it uses the current branch.\n\n## Example: move a branch off another feature branch\n\nYou branched `feature-b` off `feature-a`, but `feature-b` doesn't depend on `feature-a`'s work. You want it based directly on `main`:\n\n```\nmain:       A---B\n                 \\\nfeature-a:        C---D\n                       \\\nfeature-b:              E---F\n```\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --onto main feature-a feature-b\n```\n\nThis means: take the commits in `feature-b` that aren't in `feature-a` (E and F) and replay them onto `main`.\n\n```\nmain:       A---B\n                |\\\nfeature-a:      | C---D\n                 \\\nfeature-b:        E'---F'\n```\n\nC and D stay on `feature-a` only. `feature-b` now holds just its own work. E' and F' are new commits with new hashes.\n\n## Other common uses\n\n- **Delete commits from the middle of a branch:**\n  `git rebase --onto HEAD~3 HEAD~1` drops the two commits `HEAD~2` and `HEAD~1`, and keeps the newest one on top.\n- **After the base branch was squash-merged or rewritten:**\n  `git rebase --onto main <old-base-sha>` replays only your own commits, so you don't get conflicts from re-applying the old base's commits.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- This rewrites history. If the branch has already been pushed, you'll need `git push --force-with-lease`, and you should let anyone else using the branch know.\n- If something goes wrong, `git rebase --abort` cancels a rebase that's still in progress. After it has finished, `git reflog` shows the old branch tip so you can reset back to it."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7546, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7546}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "db55d650-87ba-4dac-bf12-918801478d1e", "uuid": "9f3fb035-eecd-45f5-911e-952ba95c9073", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T17:32:27.669Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbv8UDyJoiZMSPMf2f5j"}
